## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design an end-to-end, event-driven AWS workflow that combines storage, NLP classification, and structured indexing. It emphasizes practical decisions about ordering, retries, and privacy controls for handling real customer data.

## Key Concepts

- Event-driven architecture with S3 and Lambda
- Amazon Comprehend for text classification and named entity recognition (NER)
- Output consolidation and DynamoDB indexing
- IAM least privilege, KMS encryption, and data privacy best practices

## Code Example

```javascript
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  // 1. Get S3 object
  const s3Object = await s3.getObject({
    Bucket: event.bucket,
    Key: event.key
  }).promise();

  const ticket = JSON.parse(s3Object.Body);

  // 2. Validate schema
  if (!ticket.ticketId || !ticket.text) {
    throw new Error('Invalid ticket schema');
  }

  // 3. Classify with Comprehend
  const classifyResult = await comprehend.classifyDocument({
    Text: ticket.text,
    EndpointArn: COMPREHEND_ENDPOINT
  }).promise();

  // 4. Extract entities
  const entitiesResult = await comprehend.detectEntities({
    Text: ticket.text,
    LanguageCode: 'en'
  }).promise();

  const topEntities = entitiesResult.Entities
    .sort((a, b) => b.Score - a.Score)
    .slice(0, 3);

  // 5. Store results in S3 (encrypted)
  const output = {
    ticketId: ticket.ticketId,
    category: classifyResult.Classes[0].Name,
    confidence: classifyResult.Classes[0].Score,
    entities: topEntities,
    timestamp: new Date().toISOString()
  };

  await s3.putObject({
    Bucket: OUTPUT_BUCKET,
    Key: `results/${ticket.ticketId}.json`,
    Body: JSON.stringify(output),
    ServerSideEncryption: 'aws:kms'
  }).promise();

  // 6. Upsert DynamoDB
  await dynamoDB.put({
    TableName: TICKETS_TABLE,
    Item: {
      ticketId: ticket.ticketId,
      s3Key: `results/${ticket.ticketId}.json`,
      category: output.category,
      confidence: output.confidence,
      entities: topEntities
    }
  }).promise();

  return { statusCode: 200, body: 'Success' };
};
```

## Architecture Considerations

| Component | Consideration |
|-----------|----------------|
| **S3 Trigger** | Use S3 Event Notifications to trigger Lambda on object creation |
| **Lambda** | Configure timeout (30s+), memory (512MB+), and VPC if needed |
| **IAM** | Least privilege: Lambda needs read on input bucket, write on output bucket, Comprehend and DynamoDB access |
| **Encryption** | Use SSE-KMS for S3 buckets; enable at-rest encryption for DynamoDB |
| **Error Handling** | Implement DLQ for failed processing; add try-catch with exponential backoff for API calls |
| **Cost** | Comprehend pricing is per request; consider batching if high volume |
| **Privacy** | Never log PII; use AWS PrivateLink for Comprehend if required |

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical ability to design an end-to-end, event-driven image analytics pipeline in AWS using beginner-friendly services (S3, Lambda, Rekognition, DynamoDB) with real-world concerns like privacy, cost, and tenancy.

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven flow triggered by S3 PUT
- Rekognition detectLabels with top N labels
- Lightweight color palette extraction in Lambda
- Descriptor JSON stored in S3 and DynamoDB upsert
- IAM least privilege, DLQ/backoff, cost awareness

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js Lambda pseudo
const rek = new AWS.Rekognition();
const s3 = new AWS.S3();
const ddb = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();

exports.handler = async (evt) => {
  const key = evt.Records[0].s3.object.key;
  const bucket = evt.Records[0].s3.bucket.name;
  const img = await s3.getObject({Bucket:bucket,Key:key}).promise();
  const labels = await rek.detectLabels({Image:{Bytes: img.Body}, MaxLabels:3, MinConfidence: 60}).promise();
  const colors = extractColors(img.Body); // simple utility
  const descriptor = { imageKey:key, labels: labels.Labels.map(l=>l.Name), colors, timestamp: Date.now(), facesDetected: labels.Labels.some(l=>l.Name==='Face') };
  await s3.putObject({Bucket: bucket, Key: key + '.json', Body: JSON.stringify(descriptor)}).promise();
  await ddb.update({TableName:'ProductImages',Key:{productId: determineProductId(key)}, UpdateExpression:'SET labels=:l, colors=:c, imageKey=:k, privacyFlag=:p', ExpressionAttributeValues:{':l':descriptor.labels, ':c':descriptor.colors, ':k':key, ':p':false}}).promise();
}
```

